{"cuisine/chinese-indonesian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/chinese-indonesian-cuisine","n":"Indonesian Chinese cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Diaspora cuisine in Indonesia","intro":[["Chinese Indonesian cuisine (Indonesian: Masakan Tionghoa-Indonesia, simplified Chinese: 印尼中华料理; traditional Chinese: 印尼中華料理; pinyin: yìnní zhōnghuá liàolǐ or (Ìn-nî) Tn̂g-lâng-chia̍k (印尼唐人食) is characterized by the mixture of Chinese with local Indonesian style. Chinese Indonesians, mostly descendant of Han, Hokkien and Hakka people, brought their legacy of Chinese cuisine, and modified some of the dishes with the addition of Indonesian ingredients, such as kecap manis (sweet soy sauce), palm sugar, peanut sauce, chili, santan (coconut milk) and local spices to form a hybrid Chinese-Indonesian cuisine. Some of the dishes and cakes share the same style as in Malaysia and Singapore, known as Nyonya cuisine by the Peranakan."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"chinese-indonesian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_Indonesian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Hainanese chicken rice","dish/hainanese-chicken-rice"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/96/Nasi_Campur_Tionghoa_Kenanga_2.jpg/500px-Nasi_Campur_Tionghoa_Kenanga_2.jpg","w":500,"h":394,"by":"Gunawan Kartapranata","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nasi_Campur_Tionghoa_Kenanga_2.jpg"}},"cuisine/sephardic-jewish-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/sephardic-jewish-cuisine","n":"Sephardic Jewish cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine"]],"sub":"Cuisine of a people","intro":[["Sephardic Jewish cuisine, belonging to the Sephardic Jews—descendants of the Jewish population of the Iberian Peninsula until their expulsion in 1492—encompasses traditional dishes developed as they resettled in the Ottoman Empire, North Africa, and the Mediterranean, including Jewish communities in Turkey, Greece, Bulgaria, North Macedonia, and Syria, as well as the Sephardic community in the Land of Israel. It may also refer to the culinary traditions of the Western Sephardim, who settled in The Netherlands, England, and from these places elsewhere. The cuisine of Jerusalem, in particular, is considered predominantly Sephardic."],["Sephardic Jewish cuisine preserves medieval traditions while also incorporating dishes developed in the regions where Sephardic Jews resettled after the expulsion. Notable dishes include bourekas (savory pastries), eggplant-based dishes, medias (halved vegetables filled with meat or cheese and cooked in tomato sauce), stuffed vegetables, agristada (a sour sauce), tishpishti (a semolina and nuts cake), baklava, and cookies such as biscochos and qurbayel. Many of these dishes' names originate from Judaeo-Spanish, Turkish, and Greek, the main languages spoken by Sephardic Jews in the diaspora."],["As with other Jewish ethnic divisions composing the Jewish Diaspora, Sephardim cooked foods that were popular in their countries of residence, adapting them to Jewish religious dietary requirements, kashrut. Their choice of foods was also determined by economic factors, with many of the dishes based on inexpensive and readily available ingredients."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sephardic_Jewish_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Sephardi: Cooking the History. Recipes of the Jews of Spain and the Diaspora, from the 13th Century to Today","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Hélène Jawhara Piñer, 2021","note":"Historian's recipe collection tracing Sephardic food from medieval Spain onward.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781644695319","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781644695319"},{"name":"The New Mediterranean Jewish Table: Old World Recipes for the Modern Home","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Joyce Goldstein, 2016","note":"Sephardic, Mizrahi and Italian Jewish recipes, published by University of California Press.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9780520284999","suggested":true,"isbn":"9780520284999"}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":3,"links":[["Apio (appetizer)","dish/apio-appetizer"],["Bulemas","dish/bulemas"],["Fritas de prasa","dish/fritas-de-prasa"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Kosher salt","ingredient/kosher-salt"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Carrots","ingredient/carrots"],["Celery","ingredient/celery"],["Celery root","ingredient/celery-root"],["Lemon juice","ingredient/lemon-juice"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Parsley","ingredient/parsley"],["Vinegar","ingredient/vinegar"],["Feta cheese","ingredient/feta-cheese"],["Kashkaval","ingredient/kashkaval"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Leeks","ingredient/leeks"],["Matzo meal","ingredient/matzo-meal"],["Potatoes","ingredient/potatoes"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/5d/Boyoz_g%C3%B6sterim.jpg/500px-Boyoz_g%C3%B6sterim.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Originally created and uploaded to the Turkish Wikipedia by tr:User:ZorCoCuK","lic":"CC0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boyoz_g%C3%B6sterim.jpg"}},"dish/crab-louie":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/crab-louie","n":"Crab Louie","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Western American cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Crab Louie salad, also known as Crab Louis salad or the King of Salads, is a type of salad featuring crab meat. The recipe dates back to the early 1900s and originates on the West Coast of the United States."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-crab-louie"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crab_Louie"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Western American cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-western-united-states"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":6,"links":[["Asparagus","ingredient/asparagus"],["Crab meat","ingredient/crab-meat"],["Hard-boiled egg","ingredient/hard-boiled-egg"],["Iceberg lettuce","ingredient/iceberg-lettuce"],["Louis dressing","ingredient/louis-dressing"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"]]},{"h":"More from Western American cuisine","links":[["Dutch baby pancake","dish/dutch-baby-pancake"],["Seattle-style hot dog","dish/seattle-style-hot-dog"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/7/73/Princess_Seafood_Restaurant_-_April_2024_-_Sarah_Stierch_04.jpg/500px-Princess_Seafood_Restaurant_-_April_2024_-_Sarah_Stierch_04.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Missvain","lic":"CC BY 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Princess_Seafood_Restaurant_-_April_2024_-_Sarah_Stierch_04.jpg"}},"dish/danish-pastry":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/danish-pastry","n":"Danish pastry","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Danish cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["A Danish pastry is a multilayered, laminated sweet viennoiserie. Like other viennoiserie, such as croissants, it is neither a bread nor a pastry, as it uses yeast-leavened dough, that is laminated like puff pastry to create a layered texture."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-danish-pastry"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Danish_pastry"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Danish cuisine","cuisine/danish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":5,"links":[["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Yeast","ingredient/yeast"]]},{"h":"More from Danish cuisine","links":[["Æbleflæsk","dish/blefl-sk"],["Æbleskiver","dish/bleskiver"],["Glögg","dish/glogg"],["Kransekage","dish/kransekage"],["Kringle","dish/kringle"],["Labskaus","dish/labskaus"],["Risalamande","dish/risalamande"],["Rødgrød","dish/r-dgr-d"],["Smørrebrød","dish/sm-rrebr-d"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/6/65/Glazed_apple_Danish.jpg/500px-Glazed_apple_Danish.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Peter Isotalo","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Glazed_apple_Danish.jpg"}},"dish/feijoada":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/feijoada","n":"Feijoada","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Feijoada is the name for varieties of bean stew with beef or pork prepared in the Portuguese-speaking world."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-feijoada"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Feijoada"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Brazilian cuisine","cuisine/brazilian-cuisine"],["Portuguese cuisine","cuisine/portuguese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":5,"links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Common beans","ingredient/common-beans"],["Phaseolus vulgaris","ingredient/phaseolus-vulgaris"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Olivia's Cuisine","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"oliviascuisine.com","note":"Brazilian-born Olivia Mesquita's blog, with a large section of Brazilian classics.","url":"https://oliviascuisine.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Easy and Delish","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"easyanddelish.com","note":"Brazilian recipe collection from a Brazilian-born home cook based in the US.","url":"https://www.easyanddelish.com/easy-brazilian-recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Food From Portugal","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"foodfromportugal.com","note":"Portuguese-run recipe site and video channel of traditional Portuguese dishes.","url":"https://www.foodfromportugal.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from Brazilian cuisine","links":[["Arabu","dish/arabu"],["Arroz carreteiro","dish/arroz-carreteiro"],["Bauru (sandwich)","dish/bauru-sandwich"],["Beijinho","dish/beijinho"],["Beirute","dish/beirute"],["Bobó de camarão","dish/bobo-de-camarao"],["Bockwurst","dish/bockwurst"],["Bolo de fubá","dish/bolo-de-fuba"],["Brigadeiro","dish/brigadeiro"],["Canja de galinha","dish/canja-de-galinha"],["Coxinha","dish/coxinha"],["Maniçoba","dish/manicoba"],["Manuê","dish/manue"],["Marmelada de Santa Luzia","dish/marmelada-de-santa-luzia"],["Pão na chapa","dish/pao-na-chapa"],["Pie","dish/pie"],["Sobá","dish/soba"],["Xis (sandwich)","dish/xis-sandwich"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/24/Feijoada_%C3%A0_brasileira_1.jpg/500px-Feijoada_%C3%A0_brasileira_1.jpg","w":500,"h":353,"by":"Melsj","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Feijoada_%C3%A0_brasileira_1.jpg"}},"dish/genfo":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/genfo","n":"Genfo","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#e46a3f","intro":[["Genfo, also known as ga’at (Tigrinya: ጋዓት, gaʻat) or marca (Oromo: marqaa) is a stiff porridge-like dish that is normally formed into a round shape with a hole in the middle for the dipping sauce, a mixture of butter and red peppers, or pulses such as sunflower, seed, nut (Carthamus tinctorius) and flax (Linum usitatissimum)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-genfo"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genfo"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Eritrean cuisine","cuisine/eritrean-cuisine"],["Ethiopian cuisine","cuisine/ethiopian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Barley","ingredient/barley"],["Wheat","ingredient/wheat"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Eleni's Kitchen Blog","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"eleniskitchen.com","note":"Recipes from Ethiopian-born caterer and cooking instructor Eleni Woldeyes near Portland; also promotes her spice and sauce shop.","url":"https://www.eleniskitchen.com/blog","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/d8/Ga%27at_food.jpg/500px-Ga%27at_food.jpg","w":500,"h":335,"by":"Stefan Alfons Tzeggai","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ga'at_food.jpg"}},"dish/goshtaab":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/goshtaab","n":"Goshtaab","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Kashmiri cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Goshtaab, also spelled goshtaba, is a traditional Kashmiri dish that holds a significant place in the region's culinary heritage. Often referred to as the \"king of Kashmiri wazwan,\" goshtab is a rich and aromatic meatball curry made from finely minced mutton, yogurt, and a blend of spices. It is typically served as a final course in the traditional multi-course Kashmiri feast known as wazwan."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-goshtaab"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goshtaab"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Kashmiri cuisine","cuisine/kashmiri-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"iKashmir Cookbook","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"ikashmir.net","note":"Kashmiri Pandit community site with a collection of traditional recipes.","url":"https://ikashmir.net/cookbook/recipes/index.html","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from Kashmiri cuisine","links":[["Katlam","dish/katlam"],["Wazwan","dish/wazwan"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/ac/Goshtaba_wazwan.jpg/500px-Goshtaba_wazwan.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Oniongas","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Goshtaba_wazwan.jpg"}},"dish/kibbeh":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/kibbeh","n":"Kibbeh","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Palestinian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Kibbeh is a popular dish in the Arab world and the Levant in particular, made of spiced lean ground meat and bulgur wheat. Kibbeh is considered to be a national dish of Lebanon and Syria."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-kibbeh"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kibbeh"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Palestinian cuisine","cuisine/palestinian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Bulgur","ingredient/bulgur"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Levantine cuisine","ingredient/levantine-cuisine"]]},{"h":"More from Palestinian cuisine","links":[["Jibneh Arabieh","dish/jibneh-arabieh"],["Maltout","dish/maltout"],["Maqluba","dish/maqluba"],["Meghli","dish/meghli"],["Musakhan","dish/musakhan"],["Ntolmás","dish/ntolmas"],["Qalayet bandora","dish/qalayet-bandora"],["Qatayef","dish/qatayef"],["Stuffed squash","dish/stuffed-squash"],["Stuffed turnips","dish/stuffed-turnips"],["Taboon bread","dish/taboon-bread"],["Zalabiyeh","dish/zalabiyeh"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/88/Kibbeh3.jpg/500px-Kibbeh3.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Basel15 at the English Wikipedia","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kibbeh3.jpg"}},"dish/ladera":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/ladera","n":"Ladera","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Greek cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Ladera, zeytinyağlı  (yemekler) (Turkish), or bil zayt (Arabic بالزيت) is a category of vegetable dishes cooked in olive oil in Greek, Turkish, and Arabic cuisines. The name in all these languages means \"with (olive) oil\"."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-ladera"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ladera"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Greek cuisine","cuisine/greek-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Dimitra's Dishes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dimitrasdishes.com","note":"Dimitra Alfieris's video channel and blog focused on classic Greek home cooking.","url":"https://dimitrasdishes.com","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"}]},{"h":"More from Greek cuisine","links":[["Avgolemono","dish/avgolemono"],["Baba ghanoush","dish/baba-ghanoush"],["Bouyiourdi","dish/bouyiourdi"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Diples","dish/diples"],["Fasolada","dish/fasolada"],["Freddo cappuccino","dish/freddo-cappuccino"],["Greek restaurant","dish/greek-restaurant"],["Greek salad","dish/greek-salad"],["İmam bayıldı","dish/imam-bay-ld"],["Kakavia (soup)","dish/kakavia-soup"],["Kalitsounia","dish/kalitsounia"],["Karydopita","dish/karydopita"],["Keşkek","dish/keskek"],["Noumboulo","dish/noumboulo"],["Psarosoupa","dish/psarosoupa"],["Spanakorizo","dish/spanakorizo"],["Taramasalata","dish/taramasalata"],["Tzatziki","dish/tzatziki"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/6/60/Turkey2002_img_0137.jpg/500px-Turkey2002_img_0137.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Gürkan Sengün","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turkey2002_img_0137.jpg"}},"dish/limpa":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/limpa","n":"Limpa","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Swedish cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Wort bread (Swedish “Vörtbröd\") is a sweet Scandinavian rye bread, associated with Swedish cuisine.   It is a yeast-leavened spice loaf, sweetened with brown sugar and molasses which comes in a large variety in regard to whether or not butter-enriched, and if spices are being used. Traditional bread spices are anise, caraway, fennel seeds, and bitter orange."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-limpa"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Limpa"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Swedish cuisine","cuisine/swedish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":5,"links":[["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Maltodextrin","ingredient/maltodextrin"],["Rye flour","ingredient/rye-flour"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Yeast","ingredient/yeast"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Scandicuisine: Swedish recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"scandicuisine.com","note":"Traditional Swedish recipes from Cecilia, a cook from a Finnish-Swedish family on Åland.","url":"https://scandicuisine.com/category/swedish-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Swedish cuisine","links":[["Biff à la Lindström","dish/biff-a-la-lindstrom"],["Blåbärssoppa","dish/blabarssoppa"],["Blodpalt","dish/blodpalt"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Flying Jacob","dish/flying-jacob"],["Fruktsoppa","dish/fruktsoppa"],["Glögg","dish/glogg"],["Hasselback potatoes","dish/hasselback-potatoes"],["Isterband","dish/isterband"],["Kebab pizza","dish/kebab-pizza"],["Korv stroganoff","dish/korv-stroganoff"],["Kroppkaka","dish/kroppkaka"],["Lutefisk","dish/lutefisk"],["Oatmeal ball","dish/oatmeal-ball"],["Ostkaka","dish/ostkaka"],["Rødgrød","dish/r-dgr-d"],["Semla","dish/semla"],["Smörgåstårta","dish/smorgastarta"],["Strong beer","dish/strong-beer"],["Surströmming","dish/surstromming"],["Swedish meatballs","dish/swedish-meatballs"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/c/cb/Vortbrod.jpg/500px-Vortbrod.jpg","w":500,"h":500,"by":"Per Bolmstedt","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vortbrod.jpg"}},"ingredient/side-dish":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/side-dish","n":"Side dish","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["A side dish, sometimes referred to as a side order, side item, or simply a side, is a food item that accompanies the entrée or main course at a meal."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-side-dish"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Side_dish"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Nasi kapau","dish/nasi-kapau"]]],["Padang food","cuisine/padang-food",[["Nasi kapau","dish/nasi-kapau"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Rice","ingredient/rice"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/9b/Flickr_stuart_spivack_173603796--Macaroni_and_cheese.jpg/500px-Flickr_stuart_spivack_173603796--Macaroni_and_cheese.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Stuart Spivack from Cleveland, Ohio, USA","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flickr_stuart_spivack_173603796--Macaroni_and_cheese.jpg"}},"dish/panella":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/panella","n":"Panella","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Sicilian cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Panella is a Sicilian fritter made with chickpea flour and other ingredients, usually including water, salt, pepper, olive oil, and finely chopped parsley. They are a popular street food in the city of Palermo and are often eaten between slices of bread or on a sesame roll, like a sandwich. Called pane e panelle, these sandwiches are usually served with a slice of lemon to be squeezed over panelle."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-panella"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Panella"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Sicilian cuisine","cuisine/sicilian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Chickpea flour","ingredient/chickpea-flour"]]},{"h":"More from Sicilian cuisine","links":[["Busiate","dish/busiate"],["Cassatella di Agira","dish/cassatella-di-agira"],["Crocchè","dish/crocche"],["Iris (pastry)","dish/iris-pastry"],["Maccu","dish/maccu"],["Pani câ meusa","dish/pani-ca-meusa"],["Parmigiana","dish/parmigiana"],["Pasta alla Norma","dish/pasta-alla-norma"],["Pasta con le sarde","dish/pasta-con-le-sarde"],["Pasta mollicata","dish/pasta-mollicata"],["Stigghiola","dish/stigghiola"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/7/7f/Panelle_in_Caltanissetta.jpg/500px-Panelle_in_Caltanissetta.jpg","w":500,"h":666,"by":"Auregann","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panelle_in_Caltanissetta.jpg"}},"ingredient/cucurbita-maxima":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/cucurbita-maxima","n":"Cucurbita maxima","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Cucurbita maxima, one of at least five species of cultivated squash, is one of the most diverse domesticated species. This species originated in South America from the wild subspecies Cucurbita maxima subsp. andreana over 4,000 years ago. Cucurbita maxima, known for modern varieties as Hubbard, Delicious, Marblehead, Boston Marrow, and Turks Turban, originated in northern Argentina near the Andes or in certain Andean valleys. Secondary centers of diversity include India, Bangladesh, Myanmar, and the southern Appalachians."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-cucurbita-maxima"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cucurbita_maxima"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Peruvian cuisine","cuisine/peruvian-cuisine",[["Picarones","dish/picarones"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Chancaca","ingredient/chancaca"],["Fig","ingredient/fig"],["Panela","ingredient/panela"],["Squash (plant)","ingredient/squash-plant"],["Sweet potato","ingredient/sweet-potato"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/3a/Cucurbita_maxima_Blanco2.320.png/500px-Cucurbita_maxima_Blanco2.320.png","w":500,"h":716,"by":"Francisco Manuel Blanco (1778 – 1845) (O.S.A.)","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cucurbita_maxima_Blanco2.320.png"}},"dish/roti-jala":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/roti-jala","n":"Roti jala","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Malay cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Roti jala, roti kirai or roti renjis is a popular Malay, Minangkabau, and Acehnese tea time snack served with curry dishes which can be found in Indonesia, Malaysia and Singapore. This is a very traditional Malay dish that is usually homemade and served at events such as weddings and festivals. It is usually eaten in sets of three to four pieces with curries, especially chicken curry, as a substitute for rice."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-roti-jala"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roti_jala"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Malay cuisine","cuisine/malay-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]},{"h":"More from Malay cuisine","links":[["Akok (food)","dish/akok-food"],["Ikan bakar","dish/ikan-bakar"],["Karas (food)","dish/karas-food"],["Ketupat","dish/ketupat"],["Lekor","dish/lekor"],["Mee siput","dish/mee-siput"],["Nasi Ayam","dish/nasi-ayam"],["Nasi kerabu","dish/nasi-kerabu"],["Nasi tumpang","dish/nasi-tumpang"],["Sata (food)","dish/sata-food"],["Tahu goreng","dish/tahu-goreng"],["Ulam (salad)","dish/ulam-salad"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/d6/Bazar_Ramadan_Sejati_Walk_20250304_05_roti_jala.jpg/500px-Bazar_Ramadan_Sejati_Walk_20250304_05_roti_jala.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Taufik","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bazar_Ramadan_Sejati_Walk_20250304_05_roti_jala.jpg"}},"dish/tanindon":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/tanindon","n":"Tanindon","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Japanese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Oyakodon, literally \"parent-and-child donburi\", is a donburi, or Japanese rice bowl dish, in which chicken, egg, sliced scallion (or sometimes regular onions), and other ingredients are all simmered together in a kind of soup that is made with soy sauce and stock, and then served on top of a large bowl of rice. The name of the dish is a poetic reflection of both chicken and egg being used in the dish."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-tanindon"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tanindon"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Rice","ingredient/rice"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Just One Cookbook","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"justonecookbook.com","note":"Namiko Chen's long-running blog and videos on Japanese home cooking.","url":"https://justonecookbook.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"RecipeTin Japan","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"japan.recipetineats.com","note":"Japanese home recipes from Yumiko, a Japan-born cook, sister site to RecipeTin Eats.","url":"https://japan.recipetineats.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Japanese cuisine","links":[["Bergh–Stoutenburgh House","dish/bergh-stoutenburgh-house"],["Chikuwa","dish/chikuwa"],["Curry bread","dish/curry-bread"],["Donburi","dish/donburi"],["Fukamushicha","dish/fukamushicha"],["Gyokuro","dish/gyokuro"],["Hitsumabushi","dish/hitsumabushi"],["Horumonyaki","dish/horumonyaki"],["Iekei ramen","dish/iekei-ramen"],["Inago no tsukudani","dish/inago-no-tsu